WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:24.900
D DAY:
On November 18, 2022, Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:31.080
Holmes is scheduled to be sentenced for running&nbsp;
one of the biggest frauds on silicon valley. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.760
you’re not familiar with Elizabeth, she was the&nbsp;
founder of an ambitious biotech company called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.700
Theranos. The company promised to revolutionize&nbsp;
blood tests by reducing blood samples to a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.820
drop a of blood. As someone who’s not the biggest&nbsp;
fan of needles, this sounds like an awesome idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.940
and it appears that millions of consumers and&nbsp;
investors agreed. At the peak, Theranos had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:01.380
raised an impressive $724 million from VCs and&nbsp;
several notable investors. This included the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:07.320
likes of Larry Ellison, the Walton family, Rupert&nbsp;
Murdoch, Carlos Slim, and Robert Kraft. From their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:13.140
perspective, Theranos was probably a no brainer&nbsp;
investment. The goal of the company was easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.180
grasp and it had extremely intelligent people&nbsp;
behind it like Elizabeth herselves. Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:24.000
was a Stanford chemical engineering drop out. I&nbsp;
mean this is the exact recipe for silicon valley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.940
success stories. But, Theranos was anything&nbsp;
but a success story. While Theranos would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:37.020
reach a valuation of up to $10 billion, it turned&nbsp;
out that Theranos was just one big house of cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:44.040
held together with lies and deception. And that&nbsp;
brings us to where we are today. Elizabeth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:50.820
her business partner, Ramesh “Sunny” Balwani are&nbsp;
each facing up to 20 years in prison and a fine of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.880
$250,000 plus restitution for each count of&nbsp;
conspiracy and wire fraud. So, here’s exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.580
how Silicon’s Valley’s biggest fraud was exposed&nbsp;
and the disastrous consequences that followed.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.200
FOOLING EVERYONE:&nbsp;

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:13.020
Taking a look back, the story of Theranos’&nbsp;
downfall dates back to early 2015. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:19.200
the company had already been around for 12 years,&nbsp;
but this doesn’t mean that Elizabeth was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.720
keep up the fraud anywhere near that long. You&nbsp;
see, Thernaos had maintained a virtually non&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:31.680
existent public image up until 2013. So, for the&nbsp;
longest time, Elizabeth didn’t have to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.260
the public of anything. She just needed to&nbsp;
convince a few dozen investors. Now, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:43.380
not easy to convince investors, but more times&nbsp;
than not, their primary objections relate to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:48.780
financial success of the company as opposed to the&nbsp;
legitimacy of the company itself. And when it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.940
to finances, this company was a grand slam. If&nbsp;
they truly did achieve what they were promising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:59.400
the company would probably be worth hundreds&nbsp;
of billions today. Combine this with several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.780
heavyweight players on both the company side&nbsp;
and the investor side, not to mention the dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:09.060
of patents, and it’s unlikely that any investor&nbsp;
considered the possibility of fraud all that much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:14.700
But, while Elizabeth was able to pull a quick&nbsp;
on one investors, her facade didn’t hold up all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.680
that well in the face of public scrutiny. Theranos&nbsp;
couldn’t put off a public reveal forever though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.560
so in September of 2013, they made their first&nbsp;
public move by partnering with Walgreens. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.340
deal entailed slowing rolling out Theranos’&nbsp;
testing equipment across Walgreens nationwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.560
While consumers had yet to really hear about this&nbsp;
company till now, it didn’t take long for many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:38.142
of them to be convinced thanks to Elizabeth’s&nbsp;
marketing efforts. She would go on a spree of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.142 --> 00:03:39.000
interviews, and publications would very much play&nbsp;
into her framing. She would appear on the covers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.820
of Fortune, Forbes, The New York Times Style&nbsp;
Magazine, and Inc. Forbes labeled her as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:50.280
youngest self made female billionaire in the world&nbsp;
and ranked her as the 110th richest American. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:55.200
Theranos’ marketing wasn’t the only thing&nbsp;
that was one point, they’re pricing was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.900
on point. Naturally, you would expect that such a&nbsp;
revolutionary product would cost an arm and a leg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.060
but it was actually relatively affordable&nbsp;
and was largely covered by many notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.920
insurance companies such as Capital BlueCross&nbsp;
and AmeriHealth Caritas. All of this resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:17.160
in 175,000 people buying 7.8 million test&nbsp;
results from Theranos. But, while Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:23.040
had the general public and investors completely&nbsp;
duped, the same could not be said about medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.260
experts. While they were massive proponents of&nbsp;
such advancements themselves, they couldn’t help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.400
but feel that Theranos was pushing the bounds&nbsp;
of what was realistically possible with current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.140
technology. One such skeptic was a Missouri-based&nbsp;
pathologist who took their concerns to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.540
investigative journalist named John Carreyrou at&nbsp;
the Wall Street Journal. As you guess, John had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:48.240
no idea what he was about to discover, but as a&nbsp;
good journalist he followed this lead and started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.460
investigating Theranos. He would eventually get in&nbsp;
contact with the former lab director of Theranos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.800
Adam Rosendorff, who would spill the beans&nbsp;
on Theranos. Over the next several months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.300
John would continue his investigation and&nbsp;
secure whistleblowers including Erika Cheung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.860
and Tyler Schultz. Despite John’s efforts&nbsp;
to keep the investigation discrete though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.320
Theranos would eventually hear about it. Elizabeth&nbsp;
would go to Rupert Murdoch who actually owns the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.180
Wall Street Journal and ask him to cancel the&nbsp;
story. But, this pressure to cancel the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:24.180
just convinced John even more that he needed to&nbsp;
publish it and that’s exactly what he would do.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:31.500
THE TRUTH COMES OUT:
On October 16, 2015, the Wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:36.480
Street Journal would post an article titled “Hot&nbsp;
Startup Theranos Has Struggled With Its Blood-Test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:43.260
Technology. Silicon Valley lab, led by Elizabeth&nbsp;
Holmes, is valued at $9 billion but isn’t using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:49.020
its technology for all the tests it offers.” The&nbsp;
article revealed how Theranos was only using their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:54.060
quote on quote “proprietary technology” for a&nbsp;
small fraction of their tests. This in itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.520
doesn’t have to be a problem per se. If they were&nbsp;
upfront about how the technology only works for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.320
small subset of exams and that they were still&nbsp;
working on expanding its scope, I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.540
would’ve been completely understandable. But,&nbsp;
instead, they decided to keep up the facade that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:14.340
their technology accomplished more than it really&nbsp;
did. They continued to collect small blood samples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:19.680
even for tests that they couldn’t complete, and&nbsp;
they would simply dilute these samples and run it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:25.680
through traditional machinery. As you would guess,&nbsp;
this often resulted it heavily skewed results. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:31.560
make things worse, their proprietary technology&nbsp;
wasn’t all that reliable in the first place, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.900
they really hadn’t accomplished much of anything.&nbsp;
Nonetheless, after this report was published,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:42.720
Elizabeth would agressively deny all allegations&nbsp;
and threaten to sue John for defamation. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.220
while Elizabeth was able to get away with lying&nbsp;
to investors and the public, she couldn’t get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:51.840
away with lying to the FDA. The FDA would go&nbsp;
ahead and conduct their own investigation which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:58.860
proved the accuracy of John’s entire report. At&nbsp;
this point, it was all but over for Theranos and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:04.980
it was just a matter of waiting for the house of&nbsp;
cards topple over. In January of 2016, the Centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:10.380
for Medicare and Medicaid Services or CMS would&nbsp;
send a warning letter to Theranos instructing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:15.420
to fix the irregularities across their testing.&nbsp;
But, within just two months, this polite warning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:20.520
turned into a threat. The CMS proposed banning&nbsp;
Elizabeth from the laboratory industry for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:25.920
years. And just three months later, this proposal&nbsp;
would turn into action. Elizabeth would of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.740
try appealing this decision, but that didn’t get&nbsp;
her anywhere. In the meantime, Walgreens would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:35.640
end their relationship with Theranos and close&nbsp;
all of their instore blood collection centers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:41.160
The FDA would also ban the use of Theranos'&nbsp;
infamous nanotainers which essentially wiped out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:46.860
Theranos’ entire business. They had no product&nbsp;
left to sell and no one willing to sell it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.920
so Theranos had no option but to start laying off&nbsp;
all their employees. At their peak in late 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:58.800
they had a total of 800 employees but by&nbsp;
April of 2018, the had less than two dozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.140
employees. And most of these employees would&nbsp;
be laid off by August as well. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.520
it was clear that even if Theranos did&nbsp;
figure out blood tests with microsamples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.860
the company’s reputation was so bad that&nbsp;
they could simply never recover. So, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:19.800
no choice left, on September 4, 2018, Theranos&nbsp;
announced that they were going to dissolve.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:23.640
THE FALLOUT:
While the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:29.880
has been shutdown for years now, the repercussions&nbsp;
of their actions are still very much present with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.860
multiple lawsuits ongoing. One of the first&nbsp;
entities to take action against Theranos was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:40.740
the state of Arizona in 2017. They alleged that&nbsp;
the company had sold 1.5 million misleading blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:46.920
tests in the state, and Theranos would end up&nbsp;
settling for $4.65 million. Arizona wasn’t the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:52.020
only one that was suing Theranos either.&nbsp;
Shareholders were of course also livid and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:57.480
wanted to recoup as much as possible. But a&nbsp;
judge would actually rule that investors were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:02.760
not eligible to pursue a class action lawsuit&nbsp;
against Thernos. You see, securities laws are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:07.680
intended to protect retail investors from public&nbsp;
companies, but Theranos wasn’t a public company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.620
and its investors weren’t retail. So, this made&nbsp;
the investors ineligible to sue under federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.720
securities laws. The investors tried getting&nbsp;
around this by suing under California state laws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.360
but the judge struck this down as well. With&nbsp;
their legal options exhausted, most Theranos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:29.280
shareholders would just write off the investment&nbsp;
as a loss and move on or settle for pennies on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:36.060
dollar. Rupert Murdoch, for example, invested $125&nbsp;
million into Theranos and ended selling back his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:41.700
shares for just $1. He did receive $4 million when&nbsp;
the other shareholders came to a settlement with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:49.500
Theranos, but he still lost $121 million or 97%.&nbsp;
And this was the same case with most investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:55.200
except Elizabeth herself who has actually been&nbsp;
living it up. After being pushed out of Theranos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:59.880
Elizabeth turned around and starting dating Billy&nbsp;
Evans who is the heir to the Evans Hotel Group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:05.460
They have since gotten married and even had&nbsp;
a kid together. They currently live in a $135&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.200
million estate in Silicon Valley. If you just&nbsp;
saw her lifestyle today, you would think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:15.780
Theranos was a massive success. But, the most&nbsp;
trippy part is that one of her neighbors is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:21.000
Larry Ellison. Imagine being scammed for tens&nbsp;
of millions if not hundreds of millions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:26.580
then the scammer moves in next door. Fortunately,&nbsp;
Larry owns two dozen properties in Malibu alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:32.700
so he can stay far away from Elizabeth, but I’d&nbsp;
be surprised if this didn’t bother him atleast a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.320
little. Anyway, while Elizabeth has been able to&nbsp;
escape investor accountability for the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:43.020
the same cannot be said about the criminal&nbsp;
lawsuits against her. In June of 2018, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:48.840
DOJ charged Elizabeth and her COO with 11 counts&nbsp;
of criminal felony. And at the beginning of 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.280
she was convicted on three charges of wire&nbsp;
fraud and one count of conspiracy to commit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:58.500
wire fraud. And that brings us to where we&nbsp;
are today. Elizabeth is currently free on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:04.860
$500,000 bond and is waiting for sentencing that’s&nbsp;
scheduled for November 18, 2022. While she faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:09.420
up to 20 years in prison, most experts doubt&nbsp;
that she’ll actually serve anywhere near that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:14.760
amount. However, it’s also unlikely that she’ll&nbsp;
be able to get away with no prison time. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.080
likely scenario is that she ends up serving&nbsp;
less than 10 years at a low security prison.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:23.100
FAKE IT TILL YOU MAKE IT:
In the end, while Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:29.940
Holmes has become infamous as one of the largest&nbsp;
fraudsters in silicon valley, she didn’t actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:35.040
do anything that out of the box. She was really&nbsp;
just trying to fake it till she made it which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.200
is extremely common in silicon valley. If she had&nbsp;
a few more years, it’s possible that she would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.880
actually developed the technology for real. And&nbsp;
even if the truth about the early days did surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:50.220
after that, it wouldn’t have done anything.&nbsp;
It would’ve probably just been some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:55.440
fine and Theranos would still be up and running.&nbsp;
This is probably what Elizabeth was hoping for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:00.780
but unfortunately, she tried this strategy in the&nbsp;
wrong industry. If she had tried this strategy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:05.700
the tech industry, she probably could’ve gotten&nbsp;
away with it. But, the reason this works in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:09.900
tech industry is because the consequences&nbsp;
of not delivering aren’t that significant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:15.600
Worst case scenario, you take a reputational&nbsp;
hit, lose clients, and might get sued. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.940
in the medical industry, Elizabeth was literally&nbsp;
playing with lives. Doctors were making life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:26.460
changing medical decisions based on test results&nbsp;
from Theranos and the consequences of this can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:32.040
never truly be measured. And this alone makes&nbsp;
Elizabeth’s actions criminal and inexcusable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:37.440
but that’s just what I think. What do you think&nbsp;
Elizabeth’s punishment should be? Comment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

